[2026-01-05 21:39:00] {"conversation_id":"e1fd11ac-28de-4460-9113-524ccd227d72","generation_id":"060906d1-4ed2-4232-9234-78ca7db20084","model":"composer-1","file_path":"/Users/tamar/.cursor/worktrees/cursor-mental-models-demo/soq/README.md","edits":[{"old_string":"# Cursor Mental Models – AI Terms Dictionary\n\nA minimal static website that explains how to think about Cursor using high-level mental models. Each mental model represents a conceptual role that Cursor can play in your development workflow.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis is a **demo project** created for a live presentation about Cursor. It demonstrates how to conceptualize Cursor's capabilities through mental models like:\n\n- Cursor as a Planner\n- Cursor as a Pair Programmer\n- Cursor as a Code Reviewer\n- Cursor as a CI Fixer\n- Cursor as a Knowledge Retriever\n- Cursor as a Refactoring Assistant\n\n## What Are Mental Models?\n\nMental models are conceptual frameworks that help you understand how to use Cursor effectively. Instead of thinking about Cursor as a single tool, you can think of it playing different roles depending on your needs. Each mental model represents a different way to interact with and leverage Cursor's capabilities.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n/\n├── index.html              # Main HTML structure\n├── styles.css             # All styling\n├── app.js                 # JavaScript logic\n├── data/\n│   └── mental-models.json # Mental model data\n└── README.md             # This file\n```\n\n## Running Locally\n\nThis is a **static website** with no build step or dependencies. To run it:\n\n1. Open `index.html` in your web browser\n2. That's it!\n\n**Note:** Due to browser security restrictions, you may need to serve the files through a local web server instead of opening the file directly. You can use:\n\n- Python: `python -m http.server 8000` (then visit `http://localhost:8000`)\n- Node.js: `npx http-server` (then visit the URL shown)\n- Or any other simple HTTP server\n\n## Features\n\n- **Interactive Card Grid**: Browse mental models as cards\n- **Search**: Filter mental models by title, description, or features\n- **Modal Details**: Click any card to see detailed information\n- **Responsive Design**: Works on desktop, tablet, and mobile devices\n- **No Dependencies**: Pure HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript\n\n## Technical Details\n\n- Plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript (no frameworks)\n- No build tools or bundlers\n- No backend or external services\n- All content loaded from JSON\n- Progressive enhancement (works without JavaScript, but shows a message)\n\n## Adding Mental Models\n\nTo add new mental models, edit `data/mental-models.json` and add entries following this structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"unique-id\",\n  \"title\": \"Model Title\",\n  \"shortDescription\": \"Brief description\",\n  \"detailedExplanation\": [\"Point 1\", \"Point 2\"],\n  \"relatedFeatures\": [\"Feature 1\", \"Feature 2\"],\n  \"docs\": [\n    { \"label\": \"Link Text\", \"url\": \"https://example.com\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Demo Context\n\nThis project is intentionally simple and static to demonstrate Cursor's capabilities in a presentation setting. It prioritizes clarity and readability over complex engineering.\n","new_string":"# Cursor Mental Models – AI Terms Dictionary\n\nA minimal static website that explains how to think about Cursor using high-level mental models. Each mental model represents a conceptual role that Cursor can play in your development workflow.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis is a **demo project** created for a live presentation about Cursor. It demonstrates how to conceptualize Cursor's capabilities through mental models like:\n\n- Cursor as a Planner\n- Cursor as a Pair Programmer\n- Cursor as a Code Reviewer\n- Cursor as a CI Fixer\n- Cursor as a Knowledge Retriever\n- Cursor as a Refactoring Assistant\n\n## What Are Mental Models?\n\nMental models are conceptual frameworks that help you understand how to use Cursor effectively. Instead of thinking about Cursor as a single tool, you can think of it playing different roles depending on your needs. Each mental model represents a different way to interact with and leverage Cursor's capabilities.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n/\n├── index.html              # Main HTML structure\n├── styles.css              # All styling\n├── app.js                  # JavaScript logic\n├── data/\n│   └── mental-models.json # Mental model data (JSON array)\n└── README.md              # This file\n```\n\n## Running Locally\n\nThis is a **static website** with no build step or dependencies. To run it:\n\n1. Open `index.html` in your web browser\n2. That's it!\n\n**Note:** Due to browser security restrictions (CORS), you may need to serve the files through a local web server instead of opening the file directly. You can use:\n\n- **Python 3**: `python -m http.server 8000` (then visit `http://localhost:8000`)\n- **Node.js**: `npx http-server` (then visit the URL shown)\n- **PHP**: `php -S localhost:8000`\n- Or any other simple HTTP server\n\n## Features\n\n- **Interactive Card Grid**: Browse mental models as cards in a responsive grid layout\n- **Real-time Search**: Filter mental models by title, description, or related features as you type\n- **Modal Details View**: Click any card to see detailed explanation, related features, and documentation links\n- **Responsive Design**: Works on desktop, tablet, and mobile devices\n- **Keyboard Navigation**: Press `ESC` to close modals\n- **No Dependencies**: Pure HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript\n\n## Technical Details\n\n- **Plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript** (no frameworks or libraries)\n- **No build tools or bundlers** (works directly in the browser)\n- **No backend or external services** (fully client-side)\n- **All content loaded from JSON** (easy to update and maintain)\n- **Progressive enhancement** (shows a message if JavaScript is disabled)\n\n## Adding Mental Models\n\nTo add new mental models, edit `data/mental-models.json` and add entries to the `mentalModels` array following this structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mentalModels\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unique-kebab-case-id\",\n      \"title\": \"Cursor as a [Role Name]\",\n      \"shortDescription\": \"Brief one-sentence description\",\n      \"detailedExplanation\": [\n        \"First detailed point\",\n        \"Second detailed point\",\n        \"Third detailed point\",\n        \"Fourth detailed point\",\n        \"Fifth detailed point\"\n      ],\n      \"relatedFeatures\": [\"Feature 1\", \"Feature 2\", \"Feature 3\"],\n      \"docs\": [\n        { \"label\": \"Documentation Link Text\", \"url\": \"https://docs.cursor.com/...\" }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Important:** Make sure the JSON is valid (no trailing commas, proper quotes). The `id` should be unique and in kebab-case (e.g., `\"my-new-model\"`).\n\n## Browser Compatibility\n\nThis site uses modern JavaScript features (ES6+) and CSS Grid. It works in:\n- Chrome/Edge (latest)\n- Firefox (latest)\n- Safari (latest)\n\n## Demo Context\n\nThis project is intentionally simple and static to demonstrate Cursor's capabilities in a presentation setting. It prioritizes clarity and readability over complex engineering. The codebase is designed to be easy to understand and modify, making it a good example of clean, maintainable code.\n"}],"hook_event_name":"afterFileEdit","cursor_version":"2.3.15","workspace_roots":["/Users/tamar/cursor-mental-models-demo"],"user_email":"t0548453823@gmail.com"}
