[2026-01-05 21:39:08] {"conversation_id":"2de414e0-49b7-4975-bb28-72cd45f5fcd1","generation_id":"111ace62-01e9-4887-9c1f-999e3eeaca77","model":"gpt-5.1-codex-low-fast","file_path":"/Users/tamar/.cursor/worktrees/cursor-mental-models-demo/egg/README.md","edits":[{"old_string":"# Cursor Mental Models – AI Terms Dictionary\n\nA minimal static website that explains how to think about Cursor using high-level mental models. Each mental model represents a conceptual role that Cursor can play in your development workflow.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis is a **demo project** created for a live presentation about Cursor. It demonstrates how to conceptualize Cursor's capabilities through mental models like:\n\n- Cursor as a Planner\n- Cursor as a Pair Programmer\n- Cursor as a Code Reviewer\n- Cursor as a CI Fixer\n- Cursor as a Knowledge Retriever\n- Cursor as a Refactoring Assistant\n\n## What Are Mental Models?\n\nMental models are conceptual frameworks that help you understand how to use Cursor effectively. Instead of thinking about Cursor as a single tool, you can think of it playing different roles depending on your needs. Each mental model represents a different way to interact with and leverage Cursor's capabilities.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n/\n├── index.html              # Main HTML structure\n├── styles.css             # All styling\n├── app.js                 # JavaScript logic\n├── data/\n│   └── mental-models.json # Mental model data\n└── README.md             # This file\n```\n\n## Running Locally\n\nThis is a **static website** with no build step or dependencies. To run it:\n\n1. Open `index.html` in your web browser\n2. That's it!\n\n**Note:** Due to browser security restrictions, you may need to serve the files through a local web server instead of opening the file directly. You can use:\n\n- Python: `python -m http.server 8000` (then visit `http://localhost:8000`)\n- Node.js: `npx http-server` (then visit the URL shown)\n- Or any other simple HTTP server\n\n## Features\n\n- **Interactive Card Grid**: Browse mental models as cards\n- **Search**: Filter mental models by title, description, or features\n- **Modal Details**: Click any card to see detailed information\n- **Responsive Design**: Works on desktop, tablet, and mobile devices\n- **No Dependencies**: Pure HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript\n\n## Technical Details\n\n- Plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript (no frameworks)\n- No build tools or bundlers\n- No backend or external services\n- All content loaded from JSON\n- Progressive enhancement (works without JavaScript, but shows a message)\n\n## Adding Mental Models\n\nTo add new mental models, edit `data/mental-models.json` and add entries following this structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"unique-id\",\n  \"title\": \"Model Title\",\n  \"shortDescription\": \"Brief description\",\n  \"detailedExplanation\": [\"Point 1\", \"Point 2\"],\n  \"relatedFeatures\": [\"Feature 1\", \"Feature 2\"],\n  \"docs\": [\n    { \"label\": \"Link Text\", \"url\": \"https://example.com\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Demo Context\n\nThis project is intentionally simple and static to demonstrate Cursor's capabilities in a presentation setting. It prioritizes clarity and readability over complex engineering.\n","new_string":"# Cursor Mental Models Demo\n\nA small, no-build website that helps non-technical audiences understand Cursor through familiar roles (planner, reviewer, problem solver, and so on). Everything runs directly in the browser with plain HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript.\n\n## Why this exists\n\nLive Cursor demos move quickly. This project gives presenters and viewers a steady reference point:\n\n- Show the most common mental models for Cursor right away.\n- Let people click into a model to see detailed talking points and official docs.\n- Keep the code small enough to read on-screen without scrolling for minutes.\n\n## Run it locally\n\n1. Download or clone the repo.\n2. Open `index.html` in any modern browser.\n\nIf your browser blocks local JSON fetches, serve the folder with a basic static server:\n\n- Python: `python -m http.server 8000`\n- Node: `npx http-server`\n- Any similar one-liner works because there is no backend.\n\n## Files at a glance\n\n```\nindex.html          → page skeleton and modal markup\nstyles.css          → layout, card grid, modal styling\napp.js              → fetch JSON, render cards, wire up search + modal\ndata/mental-models.json → all copy lives here as structured data\nREADME.md           → you are here\n```\n\n## Data format\n\nEvery mental model entry in `data/mental-models.json` follows the same shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"cursor-as-a-planner\",\n  \"title\": \"Cursor as a Planner\",\n  \"shortDescription\": \"One sentence summary.\",\n  \"detailedExplanation\": [\n    \"Five bullet points that expand on the role.\"\n  ],\n  \"relatedFeatures\": [\"Feature A\", \"Feature B\"],\n  \"docs\": [\n    { \"label\": \"Cursor Docs Page\", \"url\": \"https://docs.cursor.com/...\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nKeep `id` values unique and in kebab-case so the modal system can reference them reliably.\n\n## Editing workflow\n\n1. Update the JSON file with new or refined mental models.\n2. Reload the page; the cards re-render automatically.\n3. Keep descriptions short and punchy—the demo audience probably watches along on a projector.\n\nBecause there is no build step, edits are immediate, and presenters can tweak content seconds before a session starts.\n\n## Key behaviors shown in the demo\n\n- Search filters titles and descriptions in real time.\n- Clicking a card opens a modal with five detailed bullets plus official documentation links.\n- The layout uses CSS Grid, so cards reflow cleanly on narrow screens without extra work.\n\nThat is all—open `index.html` and start presenting. ***\n"}],"hook_event_name":"afterFileEdit","cursor_version":"2.3.15","workspace_roots":["/Users/tamar/cursor-mental-models-demo"],"user_email":"t0548453823@gmail.com"}
